    <!-- About -->
    <string name="about_title">About Prosary</string>
    <string name="about_tagline">A companion for praying the Rosary and other Catholic devotions.</string>
    <string name="about_typefaces">Typefaces</string>
    <string name="about_typeface_frank_ruhl">" — SIL Open Font License 1.1 — Hebrew prayers."</string>
    <string name="about_typeface_shofar">" — GPL v2 with font-embedding exception — Hebrew Scripture (Culmus Project, Yoram Gnat)."</string>
    <string name="about_typeface_amiri">" — SIL Open Font License 1.1 — Arabic prayers."</string>
    <string name="about_typeface_scheherazade">" — SIL Open Font License 1.1 — Arabic Scripture (SIL)."</string>
    <string name="about_typeface_cardo">" — SIL Open Font License 1.1 — Latin/English Scripture (David J. Perry)."</string>
    <string name="about_typefaces_footer">Latin-script prayers use the platform\'s system serif design — not bundled with the app.</string>
    <string name="about_mystery_illustrations">Mystery Illustrations</string>
    <string name="about_mystery_intro">All 20 mystery images are classical paintings in the public domain (artist deceased over 100 years), sourced from Wikimedia Commons.</string>
    <string-array name="about_mystery_attributions">
        <item>*The Annunciation* — Fra Angelico (d. 1455)</item>
        <item>*The Visitation* — Mariotto Albertinelli (d. 1515)</item>
        <item>*The Holy Night* — Antonio da Correggio (d. 1534)</item>
        <item>*The Presentation at the Temple* — Andrea Mantegna (d. 1506)</item>
        <item>*Christ Discovered in the Temple* — Simone Martini (d. 1344)</item>
        <item>*The Baptism of Christ* — Piero della Francesca (d. 1492)</item>
        <item>*The Wedding at Cana* — Paolo Veronese (d. 1588)</item>
        <item>*The Sermon on the Mount* — Cosimo Rosselli (d. 1507)</item>
        <item>*The Transfiguration* — Raphael (d. 1520)</item>
        <item>*The Last Supper* — Leonardo da Vinci (d. 1519)</item>
        <item>*The Agony in the Garden* — Andrea Mantegna (d. 1506)</item>
        <item>*The Flagellation of Christ* — Caravaggio (d. 1610)</item>
        <item>*The Crowning with Thorns* — Caravaggio (d. 1610)</item>
        <item>*Christ Carrying the Cross* — Titian (d. 1576)</item>
        <item>*Christ Crucified* — Diego Velázquez (d. 1660)</item>
        <item>*The Resurrection* — Piero della Francesca (d. 1492)</item>
        <item>*The Ascension of Christ* — Rembrandt (d. 1669)</item>
        <item>*The Pentecost* — El Greco (d. 1614)</item>
        <item>*Assumption of the Virgin* — Titian (d. 1576)</item>
        <item>*Coronation of the Virgin* — Diego Velázquez (d. 1660)</item>
    </string-array>
    <string name="about_eastern_icons">The collection of illustrations of the Mysteries in the Eastern style is used with approval from the St. Gamliel Apostolate for Hebrew Catholics in the Aramaic (Syriac) Catholic Church.</string>
    <string name="about_other_images">Other Images</string>
    <string-array name="about_other_image_attributions">
        <item>*Crucifix* — Cimabue (d. 1302).</item>
        <item>*The Small Cowper Madonna* — Raphael (d. 1520).</item>
        <item>*Faith, Hope, and Charity* — Raphael, Baglioni altarpiece predella (d. 1520).</item>
        <item>*Praying Hands* — Albrecht Dürer (d. 1528).</item>
        <item>*Holy Trinity* — Masaccio (d. 1428).</item>
        <item>*Christ in Limbo* — Fra Angelico (d. 1455).</item>
        <item>*Michael* — Guido Reni (d. 1642).</item>
        <item>*Head of Christ* — Rembrandt (d. 1669).</item>
    </string-array>
    <string name="about_stations_illustrations">Stations of the Cross Illustrations</string>
    <string name="about_stations_fugel">All 14 stations: Gebhard Fugel (1863–1939), Kreuzweg (1921), St. Antonius, Bad Saulgau — public domain; photographs by Andreas Praefcke, released into the public domain.</string>
    <string name="about_stations_scriptural">The scriptural (St. John Paul II) form adds: The Kiss of Judas — Giotto (Scrovegni Chapel, c. 1305); Christ before the High Priest — Gerrit van Honthorst (c. 1617), National Gallery, London; The Denial of St Peter — Rembrandt (1660), Rijksmuseum; Le Coup de Lance — Peter Paul Rubens (1620), Royal Museum of Fine Arts Antwerp — all public domain. Its other scenes reuse illustrations listed elsewhere on this page.</string>
    <string name="about_stations_via_lucis">The Via Lucis scenes: The Disciples at the Tomb — Eugène Burnand (1898), Musée d\'Orsay; Noli me tangere — Fra Angelico (San Marco, c. 1440); The Road to Emmaus, the appearances to the apostles, at Lake Tiberias, and in Galilee — Duccio di Buoninsegna (Maestà, 1308–1311), Siena; Supper at Emmaus (1601) and The Incredulity of Saint Thomas (1601–1602) — Caravaggio; Christ\'s Charge to Peter — Raphael (c. 1515), Royal Collection; The Virgin in Prayer — Sassoferrato (1640–1650), National Gallery, London — all public domain. Its other scenes reuse the Rosary\'s glorious-mystery illustrations.</string>
    <string name="about_crown_illustration">Franciscan Crown Illustration</string>
    <string name="about_crown_magi">The Adoration of the Magi: Bartolomé Esteban Murillo (c. 1655–60), Toledo Museum of Art — public domain. The other six Joys reuse the Rosary mystery illustrations above.</string>
    <string name="about_sorrows_illustrations">Seven Sorrows Illustrations</string>
    <string-array name="about_sorrows_attributions">
        <item>*The Prophecy of Simeon* — Rembrandt van Rijn, Simeon’s Song of Praise (1631), Mauritshuis — public domain.</item>
        <item>*The Flight into Egypt* — Bartolomé Esteban Murillo (c. 1647–50), Detroit Institute of Arts — public domain.</item>
        <item>*The Loss of Jesus in the Temple* — Paolo Veronese, Christ Among the Doctors (c. 1560), Museo del Prado — public domain.</item>
        <item>*The Meeting on the Way of the Cross* — Raphael, Lo Spasimo (c. 1514–16), Museo del Prado — public domain.</item>
        <item>*The Crucifixion* — Hendrick ter Brugghen, The Crucifixion with the Virgin and Saint John (c. 1625), Metropolitan Museum of Art — CC0.</item>
        <item>*The Descent from the Cross* — Peter Paul Rubens (c. 1612–14), Cathedral of Our Lady, Antwerp — public domain.</item>
        <item>*The Burial of Jesus* — Titian, The Entombment of Christ (c. 1520), Musée du Louvre — public domain.</item>
    </string-array>
    <string name="about_mercy_illustration">Divine Mercy Illustration</string>
    <string name="about_mercy_kazimirowski">Eugeniusz Kazimirowski, Divine Mercy (“Jezu, ufam Tobie”, 1934), Divine Mercy Sanctuary, Vilnius — the original image painted under St. Faustina’s direction; public domain.</string>
    <string name="about_jesus_prayer_illustration">Jesus Prayer Illustration</string>
    <string name="about_jesus_pantocrator">Christ Pantocrator: encaustic icon (6th century), Saint Catherine’s Monastery, Mount Sinai — the oldest surviving icon of Christ Pantocrator, honoring the prayer’s Eastern tradition; public domain.</string>
    <string name="about_scripture_sources">Scripture Sources</string>
    <string name="about_scripture_text">Scripture quotations use the Douay-Rheims Bible (English), the Clementine Vulgate (Latin), Franz Delitzsch\'s Hebrew New Testament translation (sourced from kirjasilta.net/ha-berit) with Hebrew Old Testament passages following the Masoretic text, the Jesuit Arabic Bible (Beirut, 1880, revised 1988), the Russian Synodal Bible (1876), and Ang Dating Biblia (Tagalog, 1905) — all public domain.</string>
